  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nCurrently, strong surface high pressure is centered over \nsouthern Canada, with low pressure well south in Central Rockies\nand Central Plains. In the upper levels, a weak upper level\ncirculation was lifting north from northern Wyoming into\nsoutheast Montana. Isentropic ascent from the Central Plains\ninto the Mondak region, combined with the forcing of the upper\nlevel low, is resulting in light snow over southwest ND. It's\nhard to tell on area webcams, but it looks like any \naccumulations are light and remaining in grassy areas. \n\nFor today. Expect the light snow over southwest ND to continue \nto work northward. Some guidance suggests that the activity may \nlift as far north as the Watford City area and possibly \nWilliston. Any snow will be very light, especially north of the \nI-94 corridor. Current snowfall early this morning will\ntransition to a mix, or all rain as daytime temperatures rise.\nBy this afternoon, the isentropic upglide extends from \nnorthwest ND into the south central so don't expect much of any \nprecip east of the Missouri River, except for south of the I-94\ncorridor, and moreso along the SD border. Any precip here today\nwill be negligible as well.\n\nTonight a second, stronger upper level low, currently over the\ncentral High Plains, will lift into the Northern Plains. This\nwill bring rain and snow to much of South Dakota, but over North \nDakota, the trend continues to be lighter qpf over south central\ninto eastern ND. Currently the NBM is highlighting the far \nsouth central and southeast ND as areas where we could see a \nquarter inch of qpf. The upper low continues to trend farther \nsouth and is more of an open wave. We may still see some light \nsnow accumulations tonight over the south central and into the \nJames River valley, but impacts look to be minimal.\n\nAnother upper low, currently just off the Pacific Northwest\ncoast, will track quickly east across the Rockies, and  into \neastern Montana on Tuesday. A pretty compact upper low lifts \nfrom northeast Montana into southern Saskatchewan Tuesday \nafternoon into Tuesday evening. With a quick shot of warm \nadvection ahead of the low within an area of steep lapse rates \nand decent synoptic scale lift, we could see a band of rain/snow\nshowers lift through the northwest portion of the state Tuesday\nafternoon. With the current track of the system, a band of \nheavier precipitation would likely track to the west of the \narea. \n\nLater in the week another upper low develops off the Pacific\nNorthwest coast. there is decent agreement in the deterministic\nmodels that we see some warmer temperatures late in the workweek\nand into the weekend, downstream of this low. A hit or miss\nshower can't be ruled out, but by and large, dry conditions are\nanticipated in the Thursday-Friday timeframe. By the time we get\nto the weekend, the uncertainty ramps up quite a bit regarding\nthe evolution of the Pacific Northwest upper low. A cluster\nanalysis does yield some clarification for the weekend into\nearly next week. Of the possible solutions, the most unlikely \noutcome looks to be a progressive trough quickly moving through. \nThe favored solution is a slower developing upper trough, but \nwhether we see more northern stream trough, or a southern \nstream trough, remains to be seen.",
